"Model: free electrons in a metal (OpenStax {#}). The drift speed v_d = I/(nqA) uses the free electron density n above; n does not change with temperature here (thermal expansion changes it by under {#}%). The random speed is the Fermi speed v_F = ħ({#}π²n)^⅓/m of the free electron model, and the mean time between collisions is τ = m/(ne²ρ), so it follows ρ(T). Electrons move in a 3D random direction and are drawn projected onto the page; ions are drawn on a square grid at the mean atomic spacing (copper's real lattice is face centered cubic). The region repeats at its edges, standing for a piece deep inside the wire. The counter counts electrons carried across the dashed plane by the drift; random motion carries electrons across both ways equally on average and is left out of the count. The electrons sit at random positions, so the count scatters about n v_d t × ({#} nm × {#} nm) like any count; after the drift has carried the electrons one full region width ({#} nm), every one has crossed exactly once.",
