"A thin resistive sheet, like the conducting paper of a field mapping lab, {#} cm by {#} cm, with two electrodes held at the battery's V and at {#} V. In a sheet the current per unit width is J = E/R_s, where the sheet resistance R_s = ρ/t is the resistivity over the thickness (ohms per square: any square of the sheet, whatever its size, has R_s between two opposite edges). In a steady state no charge piles up anywhere, so as much current leaves every bit of sheet as enters it; no current crosses the sheet's edges or a cut. The demo solves that exactly on a grid of {#} cm squares (Kirchhoff's rule at every square, conjugate gradients to {#}⁻¹⁰ of V), so the map is the true answer for a sheet made of those squares; for two full width bars it gives R = R_s L/W exactly. Silver paint is modeled as {#}⁴ times as conductive as the sheet. The tint and the lines every V/{#} are equipotentials; the arrows are lines of current, one for each equal share of the current, so they crowd where J is large; they cross the equipotentials at right angles, and they are not drawn inside silver paint, which carries the current along with almost no field. R_s is a setting, not a measured paper. Press the sheet with Probe to read the potential there (the arrow keys move the probe {#} mm, Shift {#} cm); with Cut, Silver paint or Mend, drag across the sheet.",
